1
00:00:05,116 --> 00:00:10,398
One day when I was busy with my books and Mr. Pocket, I received a note by the post,

2
00:00:10,398 --> 00:00:15,519
the mere outside of which threw me into a great flutter; for, though I had never seen the

3
00:00:15,519 --> 00:00:22,319
handwriting in which it was addressed, I divined whose hand it was. It had no set beginning,

4
00:00:22,319 --> 00:00:28,639
as Dear Mr. Pip, or Dear Pip, or Dear Sir, or Dear Anything, but ran thus:—

5
00:00:28,641 --> 00:00:33,199
“I am to come to London the day after to-morrow by the midday coach. I believe

6
00:00:33,199 --> 00:00:38,160
it was settled you should meet me? At all events Miss Havisham has that impression,

7
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:42,680
and I write in obedience to it. She sends you her regard.

8
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:44,459
“Yours, Estella.”

9
00:00:44,459 --> 00:00:45,438
-

10
00:00:45,438 --> 00:00:49,758
If there had been time, I should probably have ordered several suits of clothes for

11
00:00:49,758 --> 00:00:57,160
this occasion; but as there was not, I was fain to be content with those I had. My appetite vanished

12
00:00:57,160 --> 00:01:03,277
instantly, and I knew no peace or rest until the day arrived. Not that its arrival brought

13
00:01:03,277 --> 00:01:09,038
me either; for, then I was worse than ever, and began haunting the coach-office in Wood Street,

14
00:01:09,038 --> 00:01:15,038
Cheapside, before the coach had left the Blue Boar in our town. For all that I knew

15
00:01:15,038 --> 00:01:19,758
this perfectly well, I still felt as if it were not safe to let the coach-office be out of my

16
00:01:19,758 --> 00:01:25,000
sight longer than five minutes at a time; and in this condition of unreason I had performed

17
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,839
the first half-hour of a watch of four or five hours, when Wemmick ran against me.

18
00:01:30,839 --> 00:01:37,839
“Halloa, Mr. Pip,” said he; “how do you do? I should hardly have thought this was your beat.”

19
00:01:37,839 --> 00:01:42,078
I explained that I was waiting to meet somebody who was coming up by coach,

20
00:01:42,078 --> 00:01:45,680
and I inquired after the Castle and the Aged.

21
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:51,718
“Both flourishing thankye,” said Wemmick, “and particularly the Aged. He’s in wonderful

22
00:01:51,718 --> 00:01:57,480
feather. He’ll be eighty-two next birthday. I have a notion of firing eighty-two times,

23
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:02,038
if the neighbourhood shouldn’t complain, and that cannon of mine should prove equal to

24
00:02:02,038 --> 00:02:07,959
the pressure. However, this is not London talk. Where do you think I am going to?”

25
00:02:07,959 --> 00:02:13,078
“To the office?” said I, for he was tending in that direction.

26
00:02:13,078 --> 00:02:19,038
“Next thing to it,” returned Wemmick, “I am going to Newgate. We are in a banker’s-parcel

27
00:02:19,038 --> 00:02:24,718
case just at present, and I have been down the road taking a squint at the scene of action,

28
00:02:24,718 --> 00:02:28,597
and thereupon must have a word or two with our client.”

29
00:02:28,597 --> 00:02:31,398
“Did your client commit the robbery?” I asked.

30
00:02:31,398 --> 00:02:35,917
“Bless your soul and body, no,” answered Wemmick, very drily. “But he is accused

31
00:02:35,917 --> 00:02:40,718
of it. So might you or I be. Either of us might be accused of it, you know.”

32
00:02:40,718 --> 00:02:43,839
“Only neither of us is,” I remarked.

33
00:02:43,839 --> 00:02:47,398
“Yah!” said Wemmick, touching me on the breast with his forefinger;

34
00:02:47,398 --> 00:02:53,718
“you’re a deep one, Mr. Pip! Would you like to have a look at Newgate? Have you time to spare?”

35
00:02:53,718 --> 00:02:58,078
I had so much time to spare, that the proposal came as a relief,

36
00:02:58,078 --> 00:03:04,078
notwithstanding its irreconcilability with my latent desire to keep my eye on the coach-office.

37
00:03:04,078 --> 00:03:09,436
Muttering that I would make the inquiry whether I had time to walk with him, I went into the office,

38
00:03:09,438 --> 00:03:15,078
and ascertained from the clerk with the nicest precision and much to the trying of his temper,

39
00:03:15,078 --> 00:03:19,879
the earliest moment at which the coach could be expected,—which I knew beforehand,

40
00:03:19,879 --> 00:03:26,199
quite as well as he. I then rejoined Mr. Wemmick, and affecting to consult my watch,

41
00:03:26,199 --> 00:03:32,038
and to be surprised by the information I had received, accepted his offer.

42
00:03:32,038 --> 00:03:35,680
We were at Newgate in a few minutes, and we passed through the lodge where

43
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:38,839
some fetters were hanging up on the bare walls among the prison rules,

44
00:03:39,519 --> 00:03:45,800
into the interior of the jail. At that time jails were much neglected, and the period of

45
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:51,237
exaggerated reaction consequent on all public wrongdoing—and which is always its heaviest and

46
00:03:51,237 --> 00:03:58,277
longest punishment—was still far off. So, felons were not lodged and fed better than soldiers (to

47
00:03:58,277 --> 00:04:03,557
say nothing of paupers), and seldom set fire to their prisons with the excusable object

48
00:04:03,559 --> 00:04:10,237
of improving the flavour of their soup. It was visiting time when Wemmick took me in,

49
00:04:10,237 --> 00:04:16,000
and a potman was going his rounds with beer; and the prisoners, behind bars in yards,

50
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:25,078
were buying beer, and talking to friends; and a frowzy, ugly, disorderly, depressing scene it was.

51
00:04:25,078 --> 00:04:29,000
It struck me that Wemmick walked among the prisoners much as a gardener might

52
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,038
walk among his plants. This was first put into my head by his seeing a shoot that

53
00:04:34,038 --> 00:04:38,961
had come up in the night, and saying, “What, Captain Tom? Are you there? Ah,

54
00:04:38,961 --> 00:04:44,480
indeed!” and also, “Is that Black Bill behind the cistern? Why I didn’t look for you these

55
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:50,718
two months; how do you find yourself?” Equally in his stopping at the bars and attending to

56
00:04:50,718 --> 00:04:57,237
anxious whisperers,—always singly,—Wemmick with his post-office in an immovable state,

57
00:04:57,237 --> 00:05:01,078
looked at them while in conference, as if he were taking particular notice of

58
00:05:01,078 --> 00:05:07,917
the advance they had made, since last observed, towards coming out in full blow at their trial.

59
00:05:07,917 --> 00:05:13,480
He was highly popular, and I found that he took the familiar department of Mr. Jaggers’s business;

60
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:18,038
though something of the state of Mr. Jaggers hung about him too, forbidding approach beyond

61
00:05:18,038 --> 00:05:24,758
certain limits. His personal recognition of each successive client was comprised in a nod,

62
00:05:24,758 --> 00:05:29,839
and in his settling his hat a little easier on his head with both hands, and then tightening the

63
00:05:29,839 --> 00:05:36,839
post-office, and putting his hands in his pockets. In one or two instances there was a difficulty

64
00:05:36,839 --> 00:05:41,800
respecting the raising of fees, and then Mr. Wemmick, backing as far as possible from the

65
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:48,718
insufficient money produced, said, “it’s no use, my boy. I’m only a subordinate. I can’t take it.

66
00:05:48,718 --> 00:05:54,438
Don’t go on in that way with a subordinate. If you are unable to make up your quantum, my boy,

67
00:05:54,438 --> 00:05:59,680
you had better address yourself to a principal; there are plenty of principals in the profession,

68
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:04,961
you know, and what is not worth the while of one, may be worth the while of another;

69
00:06:04,961 --> 00:06:09,879
that’s my recommendation to you, speaking as a subordinate. Don’t try on useless

70
00:06:09,879 --> 00:06:17,078
measures. Why should you? Now, who’s next?” Thus, we walked through Wemmick’s greenhouse,

71
00:06:17,078 --> 00:06:23,718
until he turned to me and said, “Notice the man I shall shake hands with.” I should have done so,

72
00:06:23,718 --> 00:06:28,519
without the preparation, as he had shaken hands with no one yet.

73
00:06:28,519 --> 00:06:34,879
Almost as soon as he had spoken, a portly upright man (whom I can see now, as I write)

74
00:06:34,879 --> 00:06:40,237
in a well-worn olive-coloured frock-coat, with a peculiar pallor overspreading the

75
00:06:40,237 --> 00:06:45,800
red in his complexion, and eyes that went wandering about when he tried to fix them,

76
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:50,199
came up to a corner of the bars, and put his hand to his hat—which had a

77
00:06:50,199 --> 00:06:57,358
greasy and fatty surface like cold broth—with a half-serious and half-jocose military salute.

78
00:06:57,358 --> 00:07:01,436
“Colonel, to you!” said Wemmick; “how are you, Colonel?”

79
00:07:01,438 --> 00:07:03,758
“All right, Mr. Wemmick.”

80
00:07:03,758 --> 00:07:08,519
“Everything was done that could be done, but the evidence was too strong for us, Colonel.”

81
00:07:09,559 --> 00:07:13,961
“Yes, it was too strong, sir,—but I don’t care.”

82
00:07:13,961 --> 00:07:19,480
“No, no,” said Wemmick, coolly, “you don’t care.” Then, turning to me,

83
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:26,358
“Served His Majesty this man. Was a soldier in the line and bought his discharge.”

84
00:07:26,358 --> 00:07:32,639
I said, “Indeed?” and the man’s eyes looked at me, and then looked over my head,

85
00:07:32,641 --> 00:07:37,277
and then looked all round me, and then he drew his hand across his lips and laughed.

86
00:07:37,277 --> 00:07:41,160
“I think I shall be out of this on Monday, sir,” he said to Wemmick.

87
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:45,000
“Perhaps,” returned my friend, “but there’s no knowing.”

88
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,680
“I am glad to have the chance of bidding you good-bye, Mr. Wemmick,” said the man,

89
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:51,879
stretching out his hand between two bars.

90
00:07:51,879 --> 00:07:57,559
“Thankye,” said Wemmick, shaking hands with him. “Same to you, Colonel.”

91
00:07:57,559 --> 00:08:00,000
“If what I had upon me when taken had been real,

92
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:05,078
Mr. Wemmick,” said the man, unwilling to let his hand go, “I should have asked the

93
00:08:05,078 --> 00:08:08,519
favour of your wearing another ring—in acknowledgment of your attentions.”

94
00:08:09,641 --> 00:08:15,961
“I’ll accept the will for the deed,” said Wemmick. “By the by; you were quite a pigeon-fancier.” The

95
00:08:15,961 --> 00:08:21,718
man looked up at the sky. “I am told you had a remarkable breed of tumblers. Could

96
00:08:21,718 --> 00:08:26,680
you commission any friend of yours to bring me a pair, if you’ve no further use for ’em?”

97
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:28,680
“It shall be done, sir.”

98
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:37,597
“All right,” said Wemmick, “they shall be taken care of. Good-afternoon, Colonel. Good-bye!” They

99
00:08:37,597 --> 00:08:44,559
shook hands again, and as we walked away Wemmick said to me, “A Coiner, a very good workman. The

100
00:08:44,559 --> 00:08:50,597
Recorder’s report is made to-day, and he is sure to be executed on Monday. Still you see,

101
00:08:50,597 --> 00:08:57,718
as far as it goes, a pair of pigeons are portable property all the same.” With that, he looked back,

102
00:08:57,718 --> 00:09:03,800
and nodded at this dead plant, and then cast his eyes about him in walking out of the yard,

103
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:08,237
as if he were considering what other pot would go best in its place.

104
00:09:08,237 --> 00:09:13,519
As we came out of the prison through the lodge, I found that the great importance of my guardian was

105
00:09:13,519 --> 00:09:19,519
appreciated by the turnkeys, no less than by those whom they held in charge. “Well,

106
00:09:19,519 --> 00:09:25,000
Mr. Wemmick,” said the turnkey, who kept us between the two studded and spiked lodge gates,

107
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:30,237
and who carefully locked one before he unlocked the other, “what’s Mr. Jaggers going to do with

108
00:09:30,237 --> 00:09:36,839
that water-side murder? Is he going to make it manslaughter, or what’s he going to make of it?”

109
00:09:36,839 --> 00:09:39,961
“Why don’t you ask him?” returned Wemmick.

110
00:09:39,961 --> 00:09:43,116
“O yes, I dare say!” said the turnkey.

111
00:09:43,116 --> 00:09:47,078
“Now, that’s the way with them here, Mr. Pip,” remarked Wemmick, turning

112
00:09:47,078 --> 00:09:51,277
to me with his post-office elongated. “They don’t mind what they ask of me,

113
00:09:51,277 --> 00:09:56,480
the subordinate; but you’ll never catch ’em asking any questions of my principal.”

114
00:09:56,480 --> 00:10:00,160
“Is this young gentleman one of the ’prentices or articled ones of your

115
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:05,116
office?” asked the turnkey, with a grin at Mr. Wemmick’s humour.

116
00:10:05,116 --> 00:10:10,160
“There he goes again, you see!” cried Wemmick, “I told you so! Asks another

117
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:16,358
question of the subordinate before his first is dry! Well, supposing Mr. Pip is one of them?”

118
00:10:16,358 --> 00:10:22,519
“Why then,” said the turnkey, grinning again, “he knows what Mr. Jaggers is.”

119
00:10:22,519 --> 00:10:27,597
“Yah!” cried Wemmick, suddenly hitting out at the turnkey in a facetious way, “you’re

120
00:10:27,597 --> 00:10:33,480
dumb as one of your own keys when you have to do with my principal, you know you are. Let us out,

121
00:10:33,480 --> 00:10:38,597
you old fox, or I’ll get him to bring an action against you for false imprisonment.”

122
00:10:38,597 --> 00:10:42,641
The turnkey laughed, and gave us good day, and stood laughing at

123
00:10:42,641 --> 00:10:48,000
us over the spikes of the wicket when we descended the steps into the street.

124
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:55,480
“Mind you, Mr. Pip,” said Wemmick, gravely in my ear, as he took my arm to be more confidential;

125
00:10:55,480 --> 00:10:58,718
“I don’t know that Mr. Jaggers does a better thing than the way in which he

126
00:10:58,718 --> 00:11:05,237
keeps himself so high. He’s always so high. His constant height is of a piece with his

127
00:11:05,237 --> 00:11:10,480
immense abilities. That Colonel durst no more take leave of him, than that turnkey

128
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:16,718
durst ask him his intentions respecting a case. Then, between his height and them,

129
00:11:16,718 --> 00:11:23,358
he slips in his subordinate,—don’t you see?—and so he has ’em, soul and body.”

130
00:11:23,358 --> 00:11:26,358
I was very much impressed, and not for the first time,

131
00:11:26,358 --> 00:11:31,678
by my guardian’s subtlety. To confess the truth, I very heartily wished,

132
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:38,519
and not for the first time, that I had had some other guardian of minor abilities.

133
00:11:38,519 --> 00:11:43,718
Mr. Wemmick and I parted at the office in Little Britain, where suppliants for Mr. Jaggers’s notice

134
00:11:43,718 --> 00:11:49,480
were lingering about as usual, and I returned to my watch in the street of the coach-office,

135
00:11:49,480 --> 00:11:55,160
with some three hours on hand. I consumed the whole time in thinking how strange it was that

136
00:11:55,160 --> 00:12:00,160
I should be encompassed by all this taint of prison and crime; that, in my childhood out on

137
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:05,639
our lonely marshes on a winter evening, I should have first encountered it; that, it should have

138
00:12:05,641 --> 00:12:11,277
reappeared on two occasions, starting out like a stain that was faded but not gone;

139
00:12:11,277 --> 00:12:20,358
that, it should in this new way pervade my fortune and advancement. While my mind was thus engaged,

140
00:12:20,358 --> 00:12:26,519
I thought of the beautiful young Estella, proud and refined, coming towards me,

141
00:12:26,519 --> 00:12:32,199
and I thought with absolute abhorrence of the contrast between the jail and her. I wished

142
00:12:32,199 --> 00:12:37,078
that Wemmick had not met me, or that I had not yielded to him and gone with him, so that,

143
00:12:37,078 --> 00:12:43,718
of all days in the year on this day, I might not have had Newgate in my breath and on my clothes.

144
00:12:43,718 --> 00:12:50,398
I beat the prison dust off my feet as I sauntered to and fro, and I shook it out of my dress, and I

145
00:12:50,398 --> 00:12:58,480
exhaled its air from my lungs. So contaminated did I feel, remembering who was coming,

146
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:03,756
that the coach came quickly after all, and I was not yet free from the soiling consciousness of

147
00:13:03,758 --> 00:13:10,358
Mr. Wemmick’s conservatory, when I saw her face at the coach window and her hand waving to me.

148
00:13:10,358 --> 00:13:15,000
What was the nameless shadow which again in that one instant had passed?
